  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 110 PM MST Thu Nov 19 2026\n\nThe positively tilted trough across the Four Corners Saturday \nmorning becomes neutral tilted as its sweeps across northern NM \nduring the day. This will result in light rain/snow across western \nand central NM Saturday morning shifting to northeast NM Saturday \nafternoon. Gusty west winds will also exist across central and \neastern NM late Saturday morning into the afternoon at the base of \nthe trough resulting in chilly and brisk conditions. High \ntemperatures will be 5 to 10 degrees below average across western \nand central NM with near average temperatures across the eastern \nplains. Skies clear and winds die shortly after sunset in the wake \nof this system setting the stage for the coldest night of the fall \nseason. Lows in the teens to near 20 are expected across northern, \nwestern and central valley location, low to mid 20s across the \nmiddle and lower RGV and northeast NM, and upper 20s to around 30 \nacross east central and southeast NM. This will result in a hard \nfreeze across almost all of the forecast zone.\n\nWeak ridging to zonal flow moves over the region Sunday. Mostly \nclear skies areawide with light winds for most areas, expect some \nlight south breezes across far northeast NM due to a weak surface \nlee low over southeast CO. Temperatures will be near normal across \nwestern and central NM and around 10 degrees below normal across \neastern NM. A weak and progressive storm system moves across the \nIntermountain West Monday. This will result in light to locally \nbreezy southwest winds across the western and central highlands and \nsouth winds across the eastern plains during the midday and \nafternoon hours. Some uncertainty exists to how far south this storm \ntracks as it moves across the Rockies Monday evening and night and \ninto the Great Plains Tuesday morning. Most of the ensemble cluster \nguidance indicates this trough either remaining just north of the \nstate or brushing northern NM bringing some very light snow showers \nto the northern mountains Monday night into early Tuesday morning. \nThe 12Z operational GFS tracking a closed low across the state \nTuesday is a major outlier. Shortwave ridging moves across the \narea mid Tuesday into Wednesday resulting in another period of \ntranquil weather. \n\nTaking an early look at Thanksgiving Day, cluster guidance is split \non two scenarios for the next storm system. The 1st and slightly \nmore unsettled scenario has the system diving southeast and \npotentially closing off into a low over New Mexico while the 2nd and \ndrier scenario keeps the system an open wave over the central \nRockies before diving southeast over the central and southern Great \nPlains east of the state Thanksgiving evening. High temperatures \nleading into and on Thanksgiving Day will be near to slightly below \naverage across western and central NM and around 10 degrees below \naverage across eastern NM.",
